        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 76.7g" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="594" ed="#liebermann"/>Hoc secundum legem et nostram consuetudinem diximus; differencia tamen weregildi multa est in Cancia uillanorum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="11" target="#Hn_76-7g.lnote.oe.11"/> et baronum.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 594 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_76-7g.lnote.oe.11" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">594</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">conuill. <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">conuill. <hi rend="italic">Lond</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_76-7g.lnote.oe._" n="-" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">327</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">nostra</hi> = Wessex; Verf. schreibt also ausserhalb Kents; <hi rend="italic">s. o</hi>. Einl. 6—77 <hi rend="italic">occisi</hi>{13} fehlt Rs</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">nostra</hi> = Wessex; the author therefore writes outside Kent; <hi rend="italic">see above</hi> Introduction 6—77 <hi rend="italic">occisi</hi>{13} missing Rs</seg></note>
